esting tidbit of history  so if elon did come from money should  that change your perception of him  i don't really think so because the vast  majority of his wealth was created  through businesses he started and grew  himself there are plenty of rich kids  who just coast through life  and never really take the time to try  and learn hard science or have an impact  on the world at  all so if you are fortunate enough to  have a wealthy family  just know that expectations are only  going to be higher for you you can't  change where you're from  so the only option is to put your head  down and build the second major  controversy in elon's career came  shortly after he sold his first company  zip2 to compaq for 307 million dollars  a physicist named john o'reilly filed a  lawsuit alleging that elon had stolen  his idea for the company  while it's true that elon had met  o'reilly before  founding zip2 it looks like o'reilly  never really pursued the idea  and was mostly looking for a piece of  elon's windfall in fact  o'reilly's company had already been  dissolved by the time he brought the  lawsuit  and the judge ultimately dismissed the  case and ordered o'reilly to pay elon  125 000 in legal fees elon never made  him pay though  so what's the takeaway for entrepreneurs  well if you get sued after selling your  company  it probably will be a headache but  you'll definitely have the money to  defend yourself  but what about people stealing your idea  in my experience  any good startup idea will be copied  iterated on and mutated  as soon as it starts getting traction  that's part of competition in the  capitalist system  and if you're an entrepreneur you should  be ready for that  you will always have competitors so your  job is to build a stronger company that  delivers a better product to its  customers  the third controversy actually came  directly out of that lawsuit  while o'reilly was digging into elon  before bringing him to court  he found that elon's degrees from the  university of pennsylvania  were granted in 1997 two years later  than elon had been stating publicly  in his biography of elon ashley vance  dug into this academic record  and found that everything actually  checked out it turned out that elon was  just missing two credits to graduate on  time  and he was planning on doing them at  stanford but after upenn  changed their graduation requirements  and elon decided not to do a phd at  stanford at all  upend let him graduate oddities in  academic records like this are  dime-a-dozen in silicon valley  dropping out is extremely trendy and  people often take gap years to work on  startups the important lesson to take  away from this is that you need to be  honest about what you've actually done  no one in the technology industry cares  if you're missing a random history  credit  but if you lie about it that will erode  trust and hurt your credibility  after elon sold zip2 he started a new  venture called  x.com in the online payment space x.com  eventually merged with confinity to  become  paypal while nearly everyone  acknowledges elon as a co-founder of  paypal now  that wasn't always the case startup  mergers can be very messy  and at the early stages it's often  unclear who's doing what  add to that the fact that elon was  ousted as ceo  after only a few months in the role and  you can see why people were unclear  about whether or not he really was a  co-founder  this all blew up in the press when  sequoia capital one of the early backers  of paypal  pulled elon's picture from their website  this could have just been them rotating  pictures  but the tech industry gossip blogs  jumped on it nevertheless  elon responded by writing a lengthy open  letter explaining exactly what he had  contributed to paypal while he was there  the most powerful part of elon's  argument to me is the fact that he was  the largest shareholder when the company  went public  typically if there's a co-founder  dispute early on and some