e to be  passionate about what you're working on  and this clearly shows that elon really  cares about advancing space travel  to be fair to the astronauts who  testified against spacex  this was back in 2012 and there was  still a lot of uncertainty about whether  or not spacex's approach of cheaper  reusable rockets would ever work  but looking back on it now they look  ridiculous spacex just delivered nasa  astronauts  to the international space station and  they did it flawlessly  elon has a unique ability to think in  decades and undertake wildly ambitious  projects  this is certainly true of spacex and  it's also true at tesla  but that has also led to many many  controversies  elon has a long history of publicly  announcing a launch date for a new tesla  model  and then missing that deadline this is  because he tends to think of timelines  as the true  minimum amount of time it would take to  do something if everything goes  perfectly  early in his career the engineers he was  working with at zip2  had a mental model for dealing with  elon's ambitious timelines  they said we came to interpret an hour  as really taking a day or two  and if elon ever did say something would  take a day  we allowed for a week or two weeks it's  good to be ambitious about timelines  when you're running a startup  since you're likely not profitable every  extra day  burns more of your cash and pushes you  closer to running out of money  but it's important to have a fundamental  reason for why  a deadline is where it is and believe  that it's actually possible to hit it  here's how elon describes it one thing i  should say like  the like when i when i sort of cite a  schedule it is actually the schedule  i think is true it's it's not some fake  schedule that i don't think is true  um maybe delusional that is entirely  you know possible maybe it's happened  from time to time  but it's it's it's never um  you know some knowingly fake deadline  ever  setting impossible deadlines will  demotivate employees  so it's important to at least have a  shot at hitting your goal  even if you know that something might  inevitably come up and delay the project  the last controversy i want to cover  involves elon's take on artificial  intelligence  unlike most silicon valley techno  optimists  elon is terrified of ai taking over  here's how he summarized his position in  2018  i'm really quite close to very close to  the cutting edge in ai and it scares the  hell out of me  um it's capable of vastly more than  almost anyone knows  and the rate of improvement is  exponential  you can see this in things like alphago  which went from  in the span of maybe six to nine months  it went from being unable to beat even a  reasonably good go player  to then beating the european world  champion who was ranked 600  then beating lisa doll four five um  who had been world champion for many  years then beating the current world  champion then beating everyone  while playing simultaneously then uh  then there was alpha zero  uh which crushed alpha go 100 to zero  and alpha zero just learnt by playing  itself  and it can play basically any game that  you put the rules in for  if you whatever rules you give it  literally read the rules play the game  and be superhuman  for any game um nobody expected that  rate of improvement if you ask those so  those same experts uh who think ai  is not progressing at the rate that i'm  saying i think you'll find that their  predictions  for things like go and and other and  other uh ai advancements have  uh the batting average is quite weak  it's not good  after elon started talking publicly  about the potential dangers of ai  there was a fair amount of backlash from  the ai industry  what i find so interesting about elon's  position is that it's very rare to see  techno pessimism  coming from within the tech industry  itself typically the loudest voices that  are critical of new technology  come from journalists academics and  executives 